  "title": "CHAPTER 19: INDIAN NATIONAL MOVEMENT (1905–1919)",
  "order": 44,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "itoslig",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "EXTREMIST PHASE, SWADESHI, HOME RULE & GANDHIAN TRANSITION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Partition of Bengal in 1905 triggered the Swadeshi and Boycott Movement, marking the transition from Moderate to Extremist politics.",
        "The Surat Split of 1907 divided the Indian National Congress into Moderates led by Gokhale and Extremists led by Tilak, Pal, and Rai.",
        "The Home Rule Movement (1916–1918) led by Bal Gangadhar Tilak and Annie Besant popularized self-government demands nationwide.",
        "The Lucknow Pact (1916) brought unity between the Congress and Muslim League, as well as the readmission of Extremists into INC.",
        "Mahatma Gandhi entered active Indian politics with early local Satyagrahas in Champaran (1917), Kheda (1918), and Ahmedabad (1918)."
      ],
      "id": "dk4pdqh"
    },
